+++
title: Uruk
description: Ancient city of Sumer and Babylonia
hook: At its peak around 3100 BC, Uruk may have had up to 50,000 residents,
  with 80,000–90,000 people living in its environs. That made it the largest
  urban area in the world at the time.
standfirst: Uruk is the type site for the Uruk period and played a leading
  role in the early urbanization of Sumer in the mid-4th millennium BC. With
  the rise of the Akkadian Empire the city lost its prime importance, and it
  was finally abandoned shortly before or after the Islamic conquest of
  633–638.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Muthanna Governorate, Iraq
    from: "location: Muthanna Governorate, Iraq"
  - name: Built
    value: c. 5000 BC
    from: |-
      history:
          built: c. 5000 BC
          abandoned: c. 700 AD
  - name: Abandoned
    value: c. 700 AD
    from: |-
      history:
          built: c. 5000 BC
          abandoned: c. 700 AD
  - name: Occupied through
    value: Uruk period to Early Middle Ages
    from: "periods: Uruk period to Early Middle Ages"
  - name: Size of the site
    value: 6 km2 (2.3 sq mi)
    from: |-
      site-notes:
          area: 6 km2 (2.3 sq mi)
  - name: Dug in
    value: 1850, 1854, 1902, 1912–1913, 1928–1939, 1953–1978, 2001–2002,
      2016–present
    from: "excavation-dates: 1850, 1854, 1902, 1912–1913, 1928–1939, 1953–1978,
      2001–2002, 2016–present"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Then Kish was defeated and the kingship was taken to Eanna (Uruk).
    attribution: Sumerian King List (SKL)
    section: list-of-rulers
  - text: Meshkiangasher entered the sea and disappeared.
    attribution: SKL
    section: list-of-rulers
  - text: 12 kings; they ruled for 2,310 years. Then Uruk was defeated and the
      kingship was taken to Ur.
    attribution: SKL
    section: list-of-rulers
  - text: For the god Iškur, lord, fearsome splendour of heaven and earth, his
      lord, for the life of Ilum-gāmil, king of Uruk, son of Sîn-irībam,
      Ubar-Adad, his servant, son of Apil-Kubi, built the Esaggianidu, ('House
      — whose closing is good'), the residence of his office of en, and
      thereby made it truly befitting his own li[fe]
    attribution: Clay cone inscription from the time of Ilum-gāmil, king of Uruk
    section: early-dynastic-akkadian-ur-iii-and-old-babylonian-period
  - text: In Uruk, in southern Mesopotamia, Sumerian civilization seems to have
      reached its creative peak. This is pointed out repeatedly in the
      references to this city in religious and, especially, in literary texts,
      including those of mythological content; the historical tradition as
      preserved in the Sumerian king-list confirms it. From Uruk the center of
      political gravity seems to have moved to Ur.
    attribution: Oppenheim
    section: political-history
timeline:
  - when: c. 5000BC
    what: The founding of Uruk, in the level called Uruk XVIII, the Eridu period.
    from: "- Uruk XVIII Eridu period (c. 5000BC): the founding of Uruk"
  - when: 4000–3200 BC
    what: "The Uruk period, or 'Uruk expansion': villages give way to a larger
      urban center with a full-time bureaucracy, military, and stratified
      society, with Uruk the main force of urbanization and state formation."
    from: In addition to being one of the first cities, Uruk was the main force of
      [[ urbanization ]] and [[ state formation ]] during the Uruk period, or
      'Uruk expansion' (4000–3200 BC). This period of 800 years saw a shift
      from small, agricultural villages to a larger urban center with a
      full-time bureaucracy, military, and stratified society. Although other
      settlements coexisted with Uruk, they were generally about 10 [[
      hectares | hectare ]] while Uruk was significantly larger and more
      complex. The Uruk period culture exported by Sumerian traders and
      colonists influenced all surrounding peoples, who gradually evolved
      their own comparable, competing economies and cultures. Ultimately, Uruk
      could not maintain long-distance control over colonies such as [[ Tell
      Brak ]] by military force.
  - when: c. 3800 BC
    what: Two centers of 20 ha (49 acres), Uruk in the south and Nippur in the
      north, stand among much smaller 10 ha (25 acres) settlements.
    from: Uruk played a very important part in the political history of Sumer.
      From the Early Uruk period onward, the city exerted [[ hegemony ]] over
      nearby settlements. At this time (c. 3800 BC), there were two centers of
      20 ha (49 acres), Uruk in the south and [[ Nippur ]] in the north
      surrounded by much smaller 10 ha (25 acres) settlements.[[ ^25 ]] Later,
      in the Late Uruk period, its sphere of influence extended over all Sumer
      and beyond to external colonies in upper Mesopotamia and Syria.
  - when: 3200 BC
    what: The inception date recorded for the city in its Wikidata entry.
    from: "inception: 3200 BC"
  - when: c. 3100 BC
    what: By the end of the Uruk period the city has reached 250 ha (620 acres).
    from: By the end of the Uruk period c. 3100 BC) Uruk had reached a size of
      250 ha (620 acres). During the following [[ Jemdet Nasr period ]] it
      grew to a size of 600 ha (1,500 acres) by c. 2800 BC with the main
      temple area of Eanna being completely rebuilt after leveling the
      foundations of the Uruk period construction.[[ ^29 ]] A new city wall
      was constructed in this period.[[ ^30 ]]
  - when: around 3000 BC
    what: The 40-to-50-foot (12 to 15 m) high brick city wall, 9 km (5.6 mi)
      around, is estimated by sedimentary strata dating to have been erected.
    from: From 1912 to 1913, [[ Julius Jordan ]] and his team from the [[ German
      Oriental Society | deutsche-orient-gesellschaft ]] discovered the temple
      of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]], one of four known temples located at the site.
      The temples at Uruk were quite remarkable as they were constructed with
      brick and adorned with colorful [[ mosaics | mosaic ]]. Jordan also
      discovered part of the [[ city wall | defensive-wall ]]. It was later
      discovered that this 40-to-50-foot (12 to 15 m) high brick wall,
      probably utilized as a defense mechanism, totally encompassed the city
      at a length of 9 km (5.6 mi). Utilizing sedimentary strata dating
      techniques, this wall is estimated to have been erected around 3000 BC.
      Jordan produced a contour map of the entire site.[[ ^30 ]]
  - when: c. 2800 BC
    what: Uruk grows to 600 ha (1,500 acres) in the Jemdet Nasr period, the main
      temple area of Eanna completely rebuilt.
    from: By the end of the Uruk period c. 3100 BC) Uruk had reached a size of
      250 ha (620 acres). During the following [[ Jemdet Nasr period ]] it
      grew to a size of 600 ha (1,500 acres) by c. 2800 BC with the main
      temple area of Eanna being completely rebuilt after leveling the
      foundations of the Uruk period construction.[[ ^29 ]] A new city wall
      was constructed in this period.[[ ^30 ]]
  - when: 27th century BC
    what: Gilgamesh rules Uruk, according to the chronology presented in the
      Sumerian King List.
    from: Uruk is the [[ type site ]] for the [[ Uruk period ]] and played a
      leading role in the early urbanization of [[ Sumer ]] in the mid-4th
      millennium BC. At its peak around 3100 BC, the city may have had up to
      50,000 residents,[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] with 80,000–90,000 people living in
      its environs,[[ ^4 ]] making it the largest urban area in the world at
      the time. [[ Gilgamesh ]], according to the [[ chronology ]] presented
      in the //[[ Sumerian King List ]]// (//SKL//), ruled Uruk in the 27th
      century BC. After the end of the [[ Early Dynastic |
      early-dynastic-period ]] period, with the rise of the [[ Akkadian Empire
      ]], the city lost its prime importance. It had periods of flourishing
      during the [[ Isin-Larsa period ]], [[ Neo-Assyrian |
      neo-assyrian-empire ]] and [[ Neo-Babylonian | neo-babylonian-empire ]]
      periods and throughout the [[ Achaemenid | achaemenid-empire ]] (550–330
      BC), [[ Seleucid | seleucid-empire ]] (312–63 BC) and [[ Parthian |
      parthian-empire ]] (227 BC to AD 224) periods, until it was finally
      abandoned shortly before or after the [[ Islamic conquest |
      muslim-conquest-of-persia ]] of 633–638. [[ William Kennett Loftus |
      william-loftus-archaeologist ]] visited the site of Uruk in 1849,
      identifying it as "Erech", known as "the second city of [[ Nimrod ]]",
      and led the first excavations from 1850 to 1854.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]
  - when: c. 2000 BC
    what: Ur collapses and Uruk goes into a steep decline.
    from: "Following the collapse of Ur (c. 2000 BC), Uruk went into a steep
      decline until about 850 BC when the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] annexed it
      as a provincial capital. Under the Neo-Assyrians and [[ Neo-Babylonians
      | neo-babylonian-empire ]], Uruk regained much of its former glory. By
      250 BC, a new temple complex, the 'Head Temple' (Akkadian: //Bīt Reš//),
      was added northeast of the Uruk-period Anu district. The //Bīt Reš//
      along with the //[[ Esagila ]]// was one of the two main centers of [[
      Neo-Babylonian astronomy | babylonian-astronomy ]]. All of the temples
      and canals were restored again under [[ Nabopolassar ]]. During this
      era, Uruk was divided into five main districts: the [[ Adad | hadad ]]
      Temple, Royal Orchard, Ištar Gate, Lugalirra Temple, and [[ Šamaš |
      shamash ]] Gate districts.[[ ^18 ]]"
  - when: about 850 BC
    what: The Neo-Assyrian Empire annexes Uruk as a provincial capital.
    from: "Following the collapse of Ur (c. 2000 BC), Uruk went into a steep
      decline until about 850 BC when the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] annexed it
      as a provincial capital. Under the Neo-Assyrians and [[ Neo-Babylonians
      | neo-babylonian-empire ]], Uruk regained much of its former glory. By
      250 BC, a new temple complex, the 'Head Temple' (Akkadian: //Bīt Reš//),
      was added northeast of the Uruk-period Anu district. The //Bīt Reš//
      along with the //[[ Esagila ]]// was one of the two main centers of [[
      Neo-Babylonian astronomy | babylonian-astronomy ]]. All of the temples
      and canals were restored again under [[ Nabopolassar ]]. During this
      era, Uruk was divided into five main districts: the [[ Adad | hadad ]]
      Temple, Royal Orchard, Ištar Gate, Lugalirra Temple, and [[ Šamaš |
      shamash ]] Gate districts.[[ ^18 ]]"
  - when: By 250 BC
    what: "The 'Head Temple' (Akkadian: Bīt Reš) is added northeast of the
      Uruk-period Anu district."
    from: "Following the collapse of Ur (c. 2000 BC), Uruk went into a steep
      decline until about 850 BC when the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] annexed it
      as a provincial capital. Under the Neo-Assyrians and [[ Neo-Babylonians
      | neo-babylonian-empire ]], Uruk regained much of its former glory. By
      250 BC, a new temple complex, the 'Head Temple' (Akkadian: //Bīt Reš//),
      was added northeast of the Uruk-period Anu district. The //Bīt Reš//
      along with the //[[ Esagila ]]// was one of the two main centers of [[
      Neo-Babylonian astronomy | babylonian-astronomy ]]. All of the temples
      and canals were restored again under [[ Nabopolassar ]]. During this
      era, Uruk was divided into five main districts: the [[ Adad | hadad ]]
      Temple, Royal Orchard, Ištar Gate, Lugalirra Temple, and [[ Šamaš |
      shamash ]] Gate districts.[[ ^18 ]]"
  - when: 200 BC
    what: The 'Great Sanctuary' of Ishtar is added between the Anu and Eanna
      districts.
    from: "Uruk, known as Orcha (Ὄρχα) to the Greeks, continued to thrive under
      the [[ Seleucid Empire ]]. During this period, Uruk was a city of
      300 hectares and perhaps 40,000 inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]][[ ^20 ]]
      In 200 BC, the 'Great Sanctuary' (Cuneiform: E₂.IRI₁₂.GAL, Sumerian:
      eš-gal) of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]] was added between the Anu and Eanna
      districts. The ziggurat of the temple of Anu, which was rebuilt in this
      period, was the largest ever built in Mesopotamia.[[ ^20 ]] When the
      Seleucids lost Mesopotamia to the [[ Parthians | parthian-empire ]] in
      141 BC, Uruk continued in use.[[ ^21 ]] The decline of Uruk after the
      Parthians may have been in part caused by a shift in the Euphrates
      River. By 300 AD, Uruk was mostly abandoned, but a group of [[ Mandaeans
      ]] settled there, based on some finds of [[ Mandaic | mandaic-language
      ]] [[ incantation bowls | incantation-bowl ]], and by c. 700 AD it was
      completely abandoned.[[ ^22 ]]"
  - when: 141 BC
    what: The Seleucids lose Mesopotamia to the Parthians; Uruk continues in use.
    from: "Uruk, known as Orcha (Ὄρχα) to the Greeks, continued to thrive under
      the [[ Seleucid Empire ]]. During this period, Uruk was a city of
      300 hectares and perhaps 40,000 inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]][[ ^20 ]]
      In 200 BC, the 'Great Sanctuary' (Cuneiform: E₂.IRI₁₂.GAL, Sumerian:
      eš-gal) of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]] was added between the Anu and Eanna
      districts. The ziggurat of the temple of Anu, which was rebuilt in this
      period, was the largest ever built in Mesopotamia.[[ ^20 ]] When the
      Seleucids lost Mesopotamia to the [[ Parthians | parthian-empire ]] in
      141 BC, Uruk continued in use.[[ ^21 ]] The decline of Uruk after the
      Parthians may have been in part caused by a shift in the Euphrates
      River. By 300 AD, Uruk was mostly abandoned, but a group of [[ Mandaeans
      ]] settled there, based on some finds of [[ Mandaic | mandaic-language
      ]] [[ incantation bowls | incantation-bowl ]], and by c. 700 AD it was
      completely abandoned.[[ ^22 ]]"
  - when: 79 or 80 AD
    what: W22340a, an astronomical almanac, is the last dated cuneiform tablet
      from Uruk.
    from: A number of [[ Proto-cuneiform ]] clay tablets were found at Uruk.
      Around 190 were Uruk V period (c. 3500 BC) "numerical tablets" or
      "impressed tablets", 1776 were from the Uruk IV period (c. 3300 BC),
      3,094 from the Uruk III period (c. 3200–2900 BC) which is also called
      the [[ Jemdet Nasr period ]].[[ ^52 ]][[ ^53 ]] Later cuneiform tablets
      were deciphered and include the famous //SKL//, a record of kings of the
      Sumerian civilization. There was an even larger cache of legal and
      scholarly tablets of the [[ Neo-Babylonian | neo-babylonian-empire ]],
      [[ Late Babylonian | akkadian-language ]], and [[ Seleucid |
      seleucid-empire ]] period, that have been published by [[ Adam
      Falkenstein ]] and other [[ Assyriological | assyriology ]] members of
      the German Archaeological Institute in Baghdad as Jan J. A. Djik,[[ ^54
      ]] [[ Hermann Hunger ]], Antoine Cavigneaux, Egbert von Weiher,[[ ^55
      ]][[ ^56 ]][[ ^57 ]][[ ^58 ]] and Karlheinz Kessler, or others as Erlend
      Gehlken.[[ ^59 ]][[ ^60 ]][[ ^61 ]] Many of the cuneiform tablets form
      acquisitions by museums and collections as the [[ British Museum ]], [[
      Yale Babylonian Collection ]], and the [[ Louvre ]]. The latter holds a
      unique cuneiform tablet in Aramaic known as the [[ Aramaic Uruk
      incantation ]]. The last dated cuneiform tablet from Uruk was W22340a,
      an astronomical almanac, which is dated to 79 or 80 AD.[[ ^62 ]]
  - when: By 300 AD
    what: Uruk is mostly abandoned, but a group of Mandaeans settle there, on the
      evidence of some finds of Mandaic incantation bowls.
    from: "Uruk, known as Orcha (Ὄρχα) to the Greeks, continued to thrive under
      the [[ Seleucid Empire ]]. During this period, Uruk was a city of
      300 hectares and perhaps 40,000 inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]][[ ^20 ]]
      In 200 BC, the 'Great Sanctuary' (Cuneiform: E₂.IRI₁₂.GAL, Sumerian:
      eš-gal) of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]] was added between the Anu and Eanna
      districts. The ziggurat of the temple of Anu, which was rebuilt in this
      period, was the largest ever built in Mesopotamia.[[ ^20 ]] When the
      Seleucids lost Mesopotamia to the [[ Parthians | parthian-empire ]] in
      141 BC, Uruk continued in use.[[ ^21 ]] The decline of Uruk after the
      Parthians may have been in part caused by a shift in the Euphrates
      River. By 300 AD, Uruk was mostly abandoned, but a group of [[ Mandaeans
      ]] settled there, based on some finds of [[ Mandaic | mandaic-language
      ]] [[ incantation bowls | incantation-bowl ]], and by c. 700 AD it was
      completely abandoned.[[ ^22 ]]"
  - when: c. 700 AD
    what: The site is completely abandoned.
    from: "Uruk, known as Orcha (Ὄρχα) to the Greeks, continued to thrive under
      the [[ Seleucid Empire ]]. During this period, Uruk was a city of
      300 hectares and perhaps 40,000 inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]][[ ^20 ]]
      In 200 BC, the 'Great Sanctuary' (Cuneiform: E₂.IRI₁₂.GAL, Sumerian:
      eš-gal) of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]] was added between the Anu and Eanna
      districts. The ziggurat of the temple of Anu, which was rebuilt in this
      period, was the largest ever built in Mesopotamia.[[ ^20 ]] When the
      Seleucids lost Mesopotamia to the [[ Parthians | parthian-empire ]] in
      141 BC, Uruk continued in use.[[ ^21 ]] The decline of Uruk after the
      Parthians may have been in part caused by a shift in the Euphrates
      River. By 300 AD, Uruk was mostly abandoned, but a group of [[ Mandaeans
      ]] settled there, based on some finds of [[ Mandaic | mandaic-language
      ]] [[ incantation bowls | incantation-bowl ]], and by c. 700 AD it was
      completely abandoned.[[ ^22 ]]"
  - when: "1835"
    what: Fraser and Ross are the first to note the location of Uruk.
    from: The location of Uruk was first noted by Fraser and Ross in 1835.[[ ^32
      ]] [[ William Loftus | william-loftus-archaeologist ]] excavated there
      in 1850 and 1854 after a scouting mission in 1849. By Loftus' own
      account, he admits that the first excavations were superficial at best,
      as his financiers forced him to deliver large museum artifacts at a
      minimal cost.[[ ^5 ]] A large basalt stela found by Loftus was later
      lost.[[ ^33 ]] Warka was also scouted by archaeologist [[ Walter Andrae
      ]] in 1902.[[ ^34 ]] In 1905 Warka was visited by archaeologist [[ Edgar
      James Banks ]].[[ ^35 ]]
  - when: "1849"
    what: William Kennett Loftus visits the site, identifying it as "Erech", known
      as "the second city of Nimrod".
    from: '[[ William Kennett Loftus | william-loftus-archaeologist ]] visited the
      site of Uruk in 1849, identifying it as "Erech", known as "the second
      city of [[ Nimrod ]]", and led the first excavations from 1850 to
      1854.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]'
  - when: 1850 to 1854
    what: Loftus leads the first excavations at Uruk.
    from: '[[ William Kennett Loftus | william-loftus-archaeologist ]] visited the
      site of Uruk in 1849, identifying it as "Erech", known as "the second
      city of [[ Nimrod ]]", and led the first excavations from 1850 to
      1854.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]'
  - when: 1912 to 1913
    what: Julius Jordan and his team from the German Oriental Society discover the
      temple of Ishtar and part of the city wall.
    from: From 1912 to 1913, [[ Julius Jordan ]] and his team from the [[ German
      Oriental Society | deutsche-orient-gesellschaft ]] discovered the temple
      of [[ Ishtar | inanna ]], one of four known temples located at the site.
      The temples at Uruk were quite remarkable as they were constructed with
      brick and adorned with colorful [[ mosaics | mosaic ]]. Jordan also
      discovered part of the [[ city wall | defensive-wall ]]. It was later
      discovered that this 40-to-50-foot (12 to 15 m) high brick wall,
      probably utilized as a defense mechanism, totally encompassed the city
      at a length of 9 km (5.6 mi). Utilizing sedimentary strata dating
      techniques, this wall is estimated to have been erected around 3000 BC.
      Jordan produced a contour map of the entire site.[[ ^30 ]]
  - when: 2016 (40th Session)
    what: Uruk Archaeological City is inscribed as part of the Ahwar of Southern
      Iraq.
    from: |2-
        unesco-world-heritage-site:
          official-name: Uruk Archaeological City
          part-of: Ahwar of Southern Iraq
          criteria: "Mixed: (iii)(v)(ix)(x)"
          reference: 1481-005
          inscription: 2016 (40th Session)
