    role: inline
    section: eanna-district
    caption: Tablet from Uruk III (c. 3200–3000 BC) recording beer distributions
      from the storerooms of an institution,[[ ^67 ]] [[ British Museum ]]
glossary:
  - term: Eanna
    gloss: The 'House of Heaven', which Enmerkar is related to have constructed
      for the goddess Inanna in the Eanna District of Uruk.
    from: "According to the //SKL//, Uruk was founded by the king [[ Enmerkar ]].
      Though the king-list mentions a father before him, the epic //[[
      Enmerkar and the Lord of Aratta ]]// relates that Enmerkar constructed
      the //[[ House of Heaven | eanna ]]// (Sumerian: //e₂-anna//; cuneiform:
      𒂍𒀭 E₂.AN) for the goddess [[ Inanna ]] in the Eanna District of Uruk.
      In the //[[ Epic of Gilgamesh ]]//, Gilgamesh builds the city wall
      around Uruk and is king of the city."
  - term: Sumerian King List (SKL)
    gloss: The king-list that Uruk's dynastic categorizations are described solely
      from, and which is of problematic historical accuracy.
    from: Dynastic categorizations are described solely from the //[[ Sumerian
      King List ]]//, which is of problematic historical accuracy;[[ ^11 ]][[
      ^12 ]] the organization might be analogous to [[ Manetho ]]'s.
  - term: Beveled rim bowls
    gloss: The most common type of container used during the Uruk period; they are
      believed to be vessels for serving rations of food or drink to dependent
      laborers.
    from: "[[ Beveled rim bowls | beveled-rim-bowl ]] were the most common type of
      container used during the Uruk period. They are believed to be vessels
      for serving rations of food or drink to dependent laborers. The
      introduction of the fast [[ wheel | potters-wheel ]] for throwing
      pottery was developed during the later part of the Uruk period, and made
      the mass production of pottery simpler and more standardized.[[ ^65 ]]"
  - term: Tripartite and T-shaped
    gloss: "The two main typologies of Sumerian architecture: Tripartite, with
      three parallel halls, and T-Shaped, with three halls, with the central
      hall extending into two perpendicular bays at one end."
    from: "Uruk has some of the first monumental constructions in architectural
      history, and certainly the largest of its era. Much of Near Eastern
      architecture can trace its roots to these prototypical buildings. The
      structures of Uruk are cited under two different naming conventions: one
      in German from the initial expedition and the English translation of the
      same. The site's stratigraphy is complex, and as a result, much of the
      dating is disputed. In general, the structures follow the two main
      typologies of [[ Sumerian architecture | architecture-of-mesopotamia ]]:
      Tripartite, with three parallel halls, and T-Shaped, with three halls,
      with the central hall extending into two perpendicular bays at one end.
      The following table summarizes the significant architecture of the Eanna
      and Anu Districts.[[ ^28 ]] Temple N, the Cone-Mosaic Courtyard, and the
      Round Pillar Hall are often referred to as a single structure: the
      Cone-Mosaic Temple."
  - term: Riemchen
    gloss: The 16 cm (6.3 in)×16 cm (6.3 in) brick shape called Riemchen by the
      Germans, first used in the Riemchen Building and then in all buildings
      of Uruk IV period Eanna.
    from: "Between these two monumental structures a complex of buildings (called
      A–C, E–K, Riemchen, Cone-Mosaic), courts, and walls was built during
      Eanna IVb. These buildings were built during a time of great expansion
      in Uruk as the city grew to 250 ha (620 acres) and established
      long-distance trade, and are a continuation of architecture from the
      previous period. The Riemchen Building, named for the 16 cm
      (6.3 in)×16 cm (6.3 in) brick shape called //Riemchen// by the Germans,
      is a memorial with a ritual fire kept burning in the center for the
      Stone-Cone Temple after it was destroyed. For this reason, Uruk IV
      period represents a reorientation of belief and culture. The facade of
      this memorial may have been covered in geometric and figural murals. The
      Riemchen bricks first used in this temple were used to construct all
      buildings of Uruk IV period Eanna. The use of colored cones as a façade
      treatment was greatly developed as well, perhaps used to greatest effect
      in the Cone-Mosaic Temple. Composed of three parts: Temple N, the Round
      Pillar Hall, and the Cone-Mosaic Courtyard, this temple was the most
      monumental structure of Eanna at the time. They were all ritually
      destroyed and the entire Eanna district was rebuilt in period IVa at an
      even grander scale."
  - term: ĝiparu
    gloss: A woven reed mat, ritually used as a nuptial bed, over which the podium
      of the Stone Temple was built.
    from: The Stone Temple was built of limestone and bitumen on a podium of [[
      rammed earth ]] and plastered with lime mortar. The podium itself was
      built over a woven reed mat called //[[ ĝiparu | giparu ]]//, which was
      ritually used as a nuptial bed. The ĝiparu was a source of generative
      power which then radiated upward into the structure. The structure of
      the Stone Temple further develops some mythological concepts from //[[
      Enuma Elish | enūma-eliš ]]//, perhaps involving libation rites as
      indicated from the channels, tanks, and vessels found there. The
      structure was ritually destroyed, covered with alternating layers of
      clay and stone, then excavated and filled with mortar sometime later.
  - term: Bīt Reš
    gloss: The 'Head Temple', added northeast of the Uruk-period Anu district by
      250 BC; with the Esagila it was one of the two main centers of
      Neo-Babylonian astronomy.
    from: "Following the collapse of Ur (c. 2000 BC), Uruk went into a steep
      decline until about 850 BC when the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] annexed it
      as a provincial capital. Under the Neo-Assyrians and [[ Neo-Babylonians
      | neo-babylonian-empire ]], Uruk regained much of its former glory. By
      250 BC, a new temple complex, the 'Head Temple' (Akkadian: //Bīt Reš//),
      was added northeast of the Uruk-period Anu district. The //Bīt Reš//
      along with the //[[ Esagila ]]// was one of the two main centers of [[
      Neo-Babylonian astronomy | babylonian-astronomy ]]. All of the temples
      and canals were restored again under [[ Nabopolassar ]]. During this
      era, Uruk was divided into five main districts: the [[ Adad | hadad ]]
      Temple, Royal Orchard, Ištar Gate, Lugalirra Temple, and [[ Šamaš |
      shamash ]] Gate districts.[[ ^18 ]]"
  - term: Mask of Warka
    gloss: A carved marble female face dating from 3100 BC, also called the 'Lady
      of Uruk' and the 'Sumerian Mona Lisa', and one of the earliest
      representations of the human face.
    from: The [[ Mask of Warka ]], also known as the 'Lady of Uruk' and the
      'Sumerian [[ Mona Lisa ]]', dating from 3100 BC, is one of the earliest
      representations of the human face. The carved marble female face is
      probably a depiction of Inanna. It is approximately 20 cm (7.9 in) tall,
      and may have been incorporated into a larger cult image. The mask was
      looted from the [[ Iraq Museum ]] during the [[ invasion of Iraq |
      2003-invasion-of-iraq ]] in April 2003. It was recovered in September
      2003 and returned to the museum.
further:
  - slug: ur
    why: Uruk kept losing the kingship to Ur on the Sumerian King List, and later
      revived as a major economic and cultural center under Ur's sovereignty.
  - slug: akkadian-empire
    why: It was the rise of the Akkadian Empire that cost Uruk its prime
      importance, and the Akkadian Empire that annexed it.
  - slug: larsa
    why: Uruk's independence in the Isin-Larsa period ran out when Irdanene was
      defeated by Rīm-Sîn I of Larsa in his year 14 (c. 1740 BC).
anchors:
  hook: At its peak around 3100 BC, the city may have had up to 50,000
    residents,[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] with 80,000–90,000 people living in its
    environs,[[ ^4 ]] making it the largest urban area in the world at the
    time.
  standfirst: Uruk is the [[ type site ]] for the [[ Uruk period ]] and played a
    leading role in the early urbanization of [[ Sumer ]] in the mid-4th
    millennium BC. At its peak around 3100 BC, the city may have had up to
    50,000 residents,[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] with 80,000–90,000 people living in its
    environs,[[ ^4 ]] making it the largest urban area in the world at the
    time. [[ Gilgamesh ]], according to the [[ chronology ]] presented in the
    //[[ Sumerian King List ]]// (//SKL//), ruled Uruk in the 27th century BC.
    After the end of the [[ Early Dynastic | early-dynastic-period ]] period,
    with the rise of the [[ Akkadian Empire ]], the city lost its prime
    importance. It had periods of flourishing during the [[ Isin-Larsa period
    ]], [[ Neo-Assyrian | neo-assyrian-empire ]] and [[ Neo-Babylonian |
    neo-babylonian-empire ]] periods and throughout the [[ Achaemenid |
    achaemenid-empire ]] (550–330 BC), [[ Seleucid | seleucid-empire ]]
    (312–63 BC) and [[ Parthian | parthian-empire ]] (227 BC to AD 224)
    periods, until it was finally abandoned shortly before or after the [[
    Islamic conquest | muslim-conquest-of-persia ]] of 633–638. [[ William
    Kennett Loftus | william-loftus-archaeologist ]] visited the site of Uruk
    in 1849, identifying it as "Erech", known as "the second city of [[ Nimrod
    ]]", and led the first excavations from 1850 to 1854.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]
