images:
  - file: Warka_vase_(background_retouched).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Warka_vase_%28background_retouched%29.jpg/250px-Warka_vase_%28background_retouched%29.jpg
    width: 2824
    height: 6632
    caption: Devotional scene to Inanna, Warka Vase, c. 3200–3000 BC, Uruk. This
      is one of the earliest surviving works of narrative relief sculpture.
  - file: Uruk_expansion.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Uruk_expansion.svg/330px-Uruk_expansion.svg.png
    width: 1185
    height: 961
    caption: Uruk expansion and colonial outposts, c. 3600–3200 BC
  - file: Uruk3000BCE.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Uruk3000BCE.jpg/250px-Uruk3000BCE.jpg
    width: 1650
    height: 826
    caption: Clay impression of a cylinder seal with monstrous lions and
      lion-headed eagles, Mesopotamia, Uruk Period (4100–3000 BC). Louvre
      Museum.
  - file: Limestone_foundation_peg_of_Lugal-kisal-si,_from_Uruk,_Iraq._C._2380_BCE._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Limestone_foundation_peg_of_Lugal-kisal-si%2C_from_Uruk%2C_Iraq._C._2380_BCE._Pergamon_Museum.jpg/250px-Limestone_foundation_peg_of_Lugal-kisal-si%2C_from_Uruk%2C_Iraq._C._2380_BCE._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    width: 3410
    height: 5171
    caption: Foundation peg of Lugal-kisalsi, king of Uruk, Ur and Kish, circa
      2380 BC. The inscription reads "For (the goddess) Namma, wife of (the
      god) An, Lugalkisalsi, King of Uruk, King of Ur, erected this temple of
      Namma". Pergamon Museum VA 4855.
  - file: Tablet_of_Sin-Gamil_of_Uruk.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Tablet_of_Sin-Gamil_of_Uruk.jpg/250px-Tablet_of_Sin-Gamil_of_Uruk.jpg
    width: 808
    height: 620
    caption: Dedication tablet of Sîn-gāmil, ruler of Uruk, 18th century BC
  - file: British_Museum_Foundation_Tablet.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/British_Museum_Foundation_Tablet.jpg/250px-British_Museum_Foundation_Tablet.jpg
    width: 1112
    height: 1752
    caption: Foundation tablet from the Temple of Inanna at Uruk (Eanna), dating
      to the reign of Ur-Nammu
  - file: Gebel_el-Arak_Knife_ivory_handle_(front_top_part_detail).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Gebel_el-Arak_Knife_ivory_handle_%28front_top_part_detail%29.jpg/250px-Gebel_el-Arak_Knife_ivory_handle_%28front_top_part_detail%29.jpg
    width: 1582
    height: 1100
    caption: Mesopotamian king as Master of Animals on the Gebel el-Arak Knife (c.
      3300–3200 BC, Abydos, Egypt), a work indicating Egypt-Mesopotamia
      relations and showing the early influence of Mesopotamia on Egypt and
      the state of Mesopotamian royal iconography in the Uruk period. Louvre.
  - file: Uruk_King-Priest_3300_BCE_portrait_detail.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Uruk_King-Priest_3300_BCE_portrait_detail.jpg/250px-Uruk_King-Priest_3300_BCE_portrait_detail.jpg
    width: 1304
    height: 1833
    caption: Probable Uruk King-Priest with a beard and hat (c. 3300 BC, Uruk).
      Louvre.
  - file: Part_of_front_of_Inanna_temple_of_Kara_Indasch_from_Uruk_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Part_of_front_of_Inanna_temple_of_Kara_Indasch_from_Uruk_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg/250px-Part_of_front_of_Inanna_temple_of_Kara_Indasch_from_Uruk_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg
    width: 2035
    height: 1580
    caption: Relief on the front of the Inanna temple of Karaindash from Uruk. Mid
      15th century BC. Pergamon Museum, Berlin.
  - file: Uruk_Temple_of_Charyos_(2).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Uruk_Temple_of_Charyos_%282%29.jpg/250px-Uruk_Temple_of_Charyos_%282%29.jpg
    width: 4752
    height: 2568
    caption: The Parthian Temple of Charyios at Uruk
  - file: Meso2mil-English.JPG
